# XSH pselect(), select() and FD_CLR(), FD_ISSET(), FD_SET(), FD_ZERO()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# select() is the same as pselect() with a null signal mask and a timeval
# timeout, so each test that is about both runs the check through both
# functions (a failure names the function). Readiness is arranged with
# pipes, FIFOs, socket pairs and a regular file. Whether a pipe is two-way
# is unspecified (pipe() page), so only the end meant for the operation is
# put in a set. The exceptional-condition set is used only for regular
# files: what counts as one for other files is implementation-defined.
# The timeout object select() may modify is never inspected. Lower bounds
# on waiting are measured with CLOCK_MONOTONIC and allow for the clock's
# resolution; no upper bound is asserted (a guard alarm gives UNRESOLVED).
# Signals for the EINTR tests come either from a child that repeats them
# or are made pending while masked, so that no test depends on timing.
# Not tested: terminals; OOB data and socket errors (not arranged
# portably); thread cancellation; an invalid timeout (the page does not say
# which intervals are invalid, see the report).

assertion  XSH.select.001
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With one pipe's read end holding data and another's empty, both
           in readfds, select() and pselect() return 1, the ready
           descriptor's bit is set on return and the other one's is clear.
test       t/001-pipe-readable.c

assertion  XSH.select.002
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       The write end of a pipe with room in it is reported ready for
           writing: select() and pselect() with it in writefds return 1 with
           its bit set.
test       t/002-pipe-writable.c

assertion  XSH.select.003
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The read end of a pipe whose write end has been closed (and which
           holds no data) is ready for reading, since a read would return
           end-of-file without blocking: select() and pselect() return 1
           with its bit set.
test       t/003-pipe-eof-readable.c

assertion  XSH.select.004
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       The return value counts the bits set over all the output sets: a
           socket with data waiting, put in both readfds and writefds,
           gives 2 from select() and from pselect(), with its bit set in
           both.
test       t/004-count-total.c

assertion  XSH.select.005
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       When the timeout expires with nothing ready (an empty pipe's read
           end in readfds, the write end of a full pipe in writefds),
           select() and pselect() return 0 and every bit of both sets is 0.
test       t/005-expiry-zero.c

assertion  XSH.select.006
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With nothing ready and no signal, select() and pselect() with a
           250 ms timeout do not return before that interval has elapsed
           (measured allowing for the clock's resolution) and then return 0.
note       Only a lower bound is asserted; the interval may be rounded up.
test       t/006-expiry-waits.c

assertion  XSH.select.007
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a zero-valued timeout the call is a poll: an empty pipe's
           read end gives 0 and a pipe with data gives 1 with its bit set;
           the call does not wait for anything (a call that does not come
           back within the guard is UNRESOLVED).
test       t/007-zero-timeout.c

assertion  XSH.select.008
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With readfds, writefds and errorfds all null and a 250 ms
           timeout, select() and pselect() wait for the interval (no return
           before it has elapsed, allowing for the clock's resolution) and
           return 0.
test       t/008-null-sets-wait.c

assertion  XSH.select.009
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A regular file always selects true for reading, for writing and
           for error conditions: with its descriptor in all three sets,
           select() and pselect() return 3 with the bit set in each.
test       t/009-regular-file.c

assertion  XSH.select.010
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The descriptors from 0 through nfds-1 are examined: a ready pipe
           whose descriptor number is the highest one below nfds (nfds is
           that number plus 1) is found, 1 is returned and its bit is set.
test       t/010-nfds-last-examined.c

assertion  XSH.select.011
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       nfds equal to FD_SETSIZE is not an error: with a ready pipe in
           readfds, select() and pselect() with nfds == FD_SETSIZE return 1.
note       The ERRORS section names nfds greater than FD_SETSIZE; the Issue
           5 change history records that equal to FD_SETSIZE was made valid.
test       t/011-nfds-fdsetsize.c

assertion  XSH.select.012
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       nfds equal to FD_SETSIZE+1 makes select() and pselect() return
           -1 with EINVAL, and the sets given are left as they were.
note       The sets are checked because the page says outright that on
           failure they are not modified.
test       t/012-einval-nfds-big.c

assertion  XSH.select.013
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A negative nfds makes select() and pselect() return -1 with
           EINVAL, and the sets given are left as they were.
test       t/013-einval-nfds-neg.c

assertion  XSH.select.014
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EBADF
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A set naming a descriptor that is not open (together with a ready
           pipe) makes select() and pselect() return -1 with EBADF, and the
           sets given are left as they were.
test       t/014-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.select.015
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EINTR
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A signal caught (handler installed without SA_RESTART) while
           select() or pselect() (null mask) waits on an empty pipe with a
           long timeout makes it return -1 with EINTR, and readfds is left as
           it was.
note       The signal comes from a child that repeats it every 20 ms, so one
           that arrives before the call cannot leave the test waiting.
test       t/015-eintr-select.c

assertion  XSH.select.016
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#ERRORS/EINTR
class      required
since      2001
says       With SIGUSR1 blocked and pending, pselect() given an empty
           sigmask and nothing ready runs the handler (installed without
           SA_RESTART) and returns -1 with EINTR instead of waiting out a
           long timeout; the caller's mask is back (SIGUSR1 blocked) on
           return, and readfds is as it was.
note       Q-87: nothing is ready here, so the unspecified case does not arise.
test       t/016-pselect-unmask-eintr.c

assertion  XSH.select.017
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A signal that the sigmask given to pselect() blocks does not cut
           the wait short: with SIGUSR1 sent every 10 ms by a child during a
           300 ms call with nothing ready, the call does not return before
           the interval has elapsed (allowing for the clock's resolution).
note       The signal may become deliverable once the original mask is
           restored, so the result (0, or -1 with EINTR) is not asserted,
           only that the call lasted the full interval.
test       t/017-sigmask-blocks.c

assertion  XSH.select.018
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       After pselect() returns, the caller's mask is back: with SIGUSR2
           blocked by the caller (on top of whatever mask the test started
           with) and a sigmask holding all signals, the mask afterwards is
           the same one (checked for a set of common signals), after a
           timeout return and after a return with a ready descriptor.
test       t/018-mask-restored.c

assertion  XSH.select.019
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       FIFOs are supported: a FIFO opened for reading (nonblocking)
           with data written by a writer is ready for reading, and the
           write end of the empty FIFO is ready for writing, in select()
           and pselect().
test       t/019-fifo.c

assertion  XSH.select.020
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Sockets are supported: one end of a socket pair is not ready for
           reading when empty, ready (return 1, bit set) after the peer
           sends a byte, and ready again, still without data, after the
           peer is closed (end-of-file), in select() and pselect().
test       t/020-socketpair.c

assertion  XSH.select.021
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A listening socket is not ready for reading with no connection
           pending, and is ready once a connection request has arrived; the
           accept() that follows completes without blocking (bounded by a
           guard that gives UNRESOLVED), in select() and pselect().
test       t/021-listening-socket.c

assertion  XSH.select.022
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A timeout of 31 days is supported: select() and pselect() with a
           ready pipe and that timeout return 1 rather than failing.
note       The page requires a maximum timeout of at least 31 days; larger
           values are cut to the maximum, so the interval itself is the
           largest one the test uses.
test       t/022-timeout-31-days.c

assertion  XSH.select.023
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A timeout does not disturb a pending alarm(): after alarm(100)
           and a select() or pselect() call that times out, alarm(0)
           reports about 100 seconds left (more than 90 and at most 100).
test       t/023-alarm-unaffected.c

assertion  XSH.select.024
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       FD_ZERO() empties a set, also one that has members: afterwards
           FD_ISSET() is zero for every descriptor from 0 to FD_SETSIZE-1.
test       t/024-fd-zero.c

assertion  XSH.select.025
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       FD_SET() adds a descriptor (FD_ISSET() is then non-zero for it and
           zero for every other one in range), also for 0 and FD_SETSIZE-1;
           adding one that is already a member changes nothing, so a single
           FD_CLR() removes it.
test       t/025-fd-set.c

assertion  XSH.select.026
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       FD_CLR() removes a member and leaves the others; on a descriptor
           that is not a member it has no effect and is not an error.
test       t/026-fd-clr.c

assertion  XSH.select.027
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/select.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       FD_ISSET() is non-zero for exactly the descriptors that are in the
           set (checked for a spread of members and non-members), and it
           accepts a pointer to a const set.
test       t/027-fd-isset.c

assertion  XSH.select.028
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null timeout, select() and pselect() wait until a
           descriptor is ready: on an empty pipe that a child fills after
           a pause, the call returns 1 with the bit set (a call that does
           not come back within the guard is UNRESOLVED).
test       t/028-null-timeout-waits.c

assertion  XSH.select.029
anchor     functions/select.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With all three sets null and a null timeout, select() and
           pselect() wait until a signal interrupts them: a signal caught
           (no SA_RESTART) from a child makes them return -1 with EINTR
           (a call that does not come back within the guard is UNRESOLVED).
note       The signal comes from a child that repeats it every 20 ms.
test       t/029-no-sets-null-timeout.c
